  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRobust cumulus field will dissipate this evening with loss of\nheating and passage of weak disturbance embedded within the\nupper level trof. Still can't rule out a spotty shower closer \nto the mountains, but this should be isolated in coverage at \nworst.\n\nExpect some dense river valley overnight though extent is in\nquestion given lack of a strong low-level inversion and a\nlingering puff in the lower boundary layer. Overnight lows will\ndrop into the 50 for most locations.\n\nWeak surface high builds across the region Thursday and Friday\nwith comfortable humidity and seasonable temperatures. Expect\nanother diurnally driven cumulus field tomorrow, aided by \nsubtle disturbances rotating southward on the back side of the \nupper level trof. \n\nUnsettled weather returns for the weekend courtesy of an upper\nlevel trof pivoting southeast into the Ohio Valley. This will\nallow for clouds to increase Friday night into Saturday with\nshowers and perhaps some storms arriving Saturday afternoon. The\ntrof axis will gradually close in on the area Sunday with\nrain chances abundant during the day. There remains \nuncertainty with just how progressive the trof axis is with \nsome model solutions advertising this axis lingers over the area\ninto Monday. This will ultimately determine the risk for excessive\nrainfall with WPC currently highlighting a marginal risk over \nparts of the area. This is something that will need to be \nmonitored over the next few days.",
